From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ep9
Subject: [PATCH] Match the participating group in a backreference to a
 shared name

ES2025 lets capture groups in different alternatives share a name
(/(?:(?<x>a)|(?<x>b))\k<x>/). \k<x> must match the text of the one
group that participated, or the empty string if none did. The parser
lowered it to an alternation of backreferences, one per group. A
backreference to a group that did not participate matches the empty
string, so the first arm won: /(?:(?<x>a)|(?<x>b))\k<x>/ matched only
"b" in "bb" (test262 built-ins/RegExp/named-groups/
duplicate-names-exec.js, -match.js, -test.js).

A new IR node and instruction, BackRefAny, holds the list of groups and
matches the first one that has a capture (both engines).

Also: without the u or v flag, a pattern with a group name parses with
[+NamedCaptureGroups], and there k is not an identity escape (Annex B
SourceCharacterIdentityEscape), so [\k] is a SyntaxError, as in V8.

Test: tests/named_backrefs.rs.
